import { PracticalCollectionCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> PracticalCollectionCopy = PracticalCollectionCopy {
  summary: "items.head returns a List’s first element in Just, or Nothing for an empty List. A first element that is an empty string is still a present result.",
  taskTitle: "Read the first label, including when the list is empty",
  purpose: "Display the first label while allowing for a list with no entries yet. The example’s first label is an empty string, so having no text to display stays distinct from having no element.",
  typescriptBefore: "For a normal array, labels[0] reads the first value and an explicit undefined check handles absence. This is already concise, idiomatic TypeScript. The comparison is the task of reading the first item; JavaScript arrays and Seseragi Lists are different data structures.",
  benefit: "head returns “use the first item if present” as Maybe<String>. Handle that one result with match instead of separating a length check from a second operation to retrieve the item.",
  setup: "items is the alias for std/list, and items.head labels passes one List argument. The backtick before [ in `[\"\", \"Review\"] creates a List. Ordinary [\"\", \"Review\"] creates an Array and cannot be passed directly to a std/list function.",
  after: [
    "The final two lines call head. labels has an empty string at its beginning, so it prints present: \"\"; the empty List made by items.empty<String> () prints missing. <String> specifies the empty List’s element type, and () is the Unit value supplied to empty; it carries no meaningful information.",
    "describe accepts Maybe<String>, either Just containing a String or Nothing without a value. Its match uses the contained value in Just label and returns missing for Nothing. json.encodeString puts quotes around even an empty string so its presence is visible.",
    "The earlier five lines use get to read the same data by position. Positions 0 and 1 exist; a negative position, the position just past the end at 2, and an empty List produce missing. head itself takes no position argument.",
  ],
  details: [
    "head only checks whether there is a first element. An empty List gives Nothing; otherwise it puts the first element in Just. It does not walk the whole List, and the work does not grow with its length.",
    "A first element of 0 or False gives Just 0 or Just False. An empty string is treated the same way. The element’s truth value or character count does not determine whether the List is empty.",
    "Maybe<String> is not String. Provide a branch that uses the Just value and a branch for absence. This example displays missing; an application can choose its own empty-state message there.",
    "Use get for a position other than the first. List get and length walk through elements, so reading just the first item does not require counting everything with length before calling get 0.",
  ],
}
